{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791433636,"nonce":"twhq4MSmKyAY/PMD7vOw2i1VGzw=","message":"The current landed baseline is 04ab5b71329d6adf05a0c4bec27ecb460bd35238, scoring 898/1094.\n\nEmptyArrayValues.hx passes native Haxe and fails pinned Cranelift with empty array element. Array\u003cAny\u003e = [12, false, \"text\", {}, 1.5] has fewer than five elements because Array AST lowering treats {} as a statement block. Array elements are value contexts, like function arguments, initializers, and return expressions, which already use lower_value_expression.\n\nEmptyAbstractValue.hx, a simple Dynamic-backed abstract with an Int property initialized from {}, passes native and pinned. The original issue's claim that all Dynamic-backed abstract initializers were lost is therefore too broad. Issue5226 still crashes, but its @:from String property conversion needs a separate diagnosis.\n\nArrayElementValues.hx adds empty object positions, a mutable empty object, nested arrays, and an ambiguous same-named enum constructor whose parent is selected by the array element type. It passes native Haxe. Prototype v1 lowers each element in a value context and supplies the expected element type rather than the outer array type; all context stacks are restored on failure.\n\nArtifacts: /tmp/rayzor-empty-array-native-v1.log, /tmp/rayzor-empty-array-before-v1.log, /tmp/rayzor-empty-abstract-native-v1.log, /tmp/rayzor-empty-abstract-before-v1.log, /tmp/rayzor-array-elements-native-v1.log.","files":null}]}